If interviewer asks suddenly, remember these 5:

1. Auto-Waiting
2. Faster Execution
3. Modern Locators (getByRole)
4. Browser Contexts
5. Trace Viewer


1. Auto-Waiting ⭐
Selenium
driver.findElement(By.id("login")).click();

Often needs:

WebDriverWait
Implicit Wait
Explicit Wait
Thread.sleep()
Playwright
await page.getByRole('button', {
  name: 'Login'
}).click();

Playwright automatically waits for:

Element to exist
Element to be visible
Element to be enabled
Element to be stable

✅ Less flaky tests


2. Faster Execution

Playwright communicates directly with browsers.

Selenium uses:

Test Script
    ↓
WebDriver
    ↓
Browser

Playwright:

Test Script
    ↓
Browser

✅ Generally faster execution.

3. Modern Locator Strategy ⭐
Selenium
driver.findElement(By.xpath("//button[text()='Login']"))
Playwright
page.getByRole('button', {
  name: 'Login'
})

Playwright encourages user-facing locators.

✅ More readable
✅ More maintainable


4. Multiple Browser Contexts

Playwright can create multiple isolated sessions in the same browser.

const context1 = await browser.newContext();
const context2 = await browser.newContext();

Useful for:

Buyer vs Seller
Admin vs User
Chat Applications

Much lighter than launching multiple browsers.

5. Trace Viewer ⭐

One of Playwright's strongest features.

After failure you can see:

Action performed
↓
Network calls
↓
Screenshots
↓
DOM state
↓
Error

Almost like a "recording" of the test.

Helps debug failures very quickly.